## Roles

- **This session** designs and orchestrates. It never explores, researches or implements the task itself, and never hands the design to another agent.
- **worker** explores, researches, implements, merges and fixes review findings.
- **reviewer** reviews read-only and diagnoses hard failures. It never reviews work it wrote.
- **committee**: read-only subagents called when the same failure happens twice, one per entry in the `committee` list (two by default); they alternate between digging for the root cause and challenging the approach.

%% cc
All of them are subagents started with Claude Code's Agent tool.
%% codex
All of them are subagents started with Codex's `spawn_agent`, without this conversation's history.
%% end

## Models

| Role | Default | Change it |
| --- | --- | --- |
%% cc
| worker | the host's subagent default (`CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` when set) | `cc.worker` in the config, or `--worker <model>[/<effort>]` |
| reviewer | `opus` | `cc.reviewer`, or `--reviewer <model>[/<effort>]` |
| committee | `opus` and `fable` | `cc.committee` |
%% codex
| worker | this session's model | `codex.worker` in the config, or `--worker <model>[/<effort>]` |
| reviewer | none: set by setup | `codex.reviewer`, or `--reviewer <model>[/<effort>]` |
| committee | none: set by setup | `codex.committee` |
%% end

%% cc
The config is `~/.native-routed/config.yaml`, written by `/native-routed-setup-{host}`. Values are whatever the Agent tool accepts for `model`. An explicit `model` overrides `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, which is how the reviewer ends up on a stronger model than the worker.
%% codex
The config is `~/.native-routed/config.yaml`, written by `$native-routed-setup-{host}`. Model names differ between machines and accounts, so the skills ship none: setup reads the models `spawn_agent` offers and suggests a strong one for the reviewer and two for the committee. Without that config the routed skills stop and point to setup.
%% end

`budget` (`small`, `medium`, `large`, `unlimited`) sets the reasoning effort of every role without its own, and `--budget <budget>` changes it for one run. `inherit` as a role's model means no model parameter. If a configured model fails to start mid-run, the run falls back in a fixed order and lists the fallback in its report.

## Common questions

%% cc
**How is this different from paseo-routed?** No Paseo daemon, profiles or rosters: roles are Claude Code subagents, and models come from one small config. In exchange, subagents end with this session and can't use another harness for review.
%% codex
**How is this different from paseo-routed?** No Paseo daemon, profiles or rosters: roles are Codex subagents, and models come from one small config. In exchange, subagents end with this session and can't use another harness for review.
%% end

**Where do notes go?** `~/.native-routed/notes/<topic>/`, outside the repo, so every subagent can read them and they never get committed. `session.json` there lets `native-watch-{host}` find the run.

**Can the reviewer write files?** The tool allows it. Review briefs state read-only, and the orchestrator checks `git status` after every review.

**A run stopped halfway.** Say "continue". Subagents don't survive a restart, so the orchestrator reads the notes and checks each worktree's `git log` and `git status`, then re-dispatches what's unfinished.

**The same failure keeps happening.** On its second occurrence the orchestrator calls the committee for an independent root-cause analysis instead of retrying.

%% cc
**The watch stopped reporting.** Its cron job lives only in the watching session, fires only while that session is idle, and expires after 7 days. Run `/native-watch-{host} start` again.
%% codex
**The watch doesn't patrol on its own.** Scheduled patrols need the `automation_update` tool, which the Codex app has and the CLI doesn't. In the CLI, run `$native-watch-{host} now` when you want a report.

**A relayed decision didn't run.** Relays use `codex queue` without `--resume`, so the message waits until you open that session. Ask the watch to resume it if you want it to run unattended.
%% end
